prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a flying dragon flight simulator. The player uses the keyboard to control a dragon flying through 3D space and scores points by passing through randomly generated ring checkpoints.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/Fox.glb. This model is a dragon model;
(2) After loading, scale the dragon to an appropriate size (scale approximately 0.3-0.8), set its initial position to (0, 20, 0), and make it face the positive z direction;
(3) If the model contains an animation (flying/wing flapping), use AnimationMixer to play the flying animation in a loop;

[Flight Controls]
(4) WASD controls the flight direction:
  - W: Dive (pitch down, rotate downward around the local x-axis)
  - S: Pull up (pitch up, rotate upward around the local x-axis)
  - A: Yaw left (turn left around the local y-axis)
  - D: Yaw right (turn right around the local y-axis)
(5) Rotation speed: approximately 1.5 rad/s each for pitch and yaw;
(6) Space key: Accelerated ascent (add an extra upward component of 5 m/s);
(7) Shift key: Accelerated dive (add an extra forward-and-downward component of 5 m/s);
(8) The dragon always has a base forward speed of 8 m/s (along its current facing direction), so it continues flying forward even when no key is pressed;

[Ring Checkpoints]
(9) Randomly generate ring checkpoints in 3D space (TorusGeometry, major radius 3, tube radius 0.3, emissive material with color 0xffaa00);
(10) Initially generate 5 rings, distributed at random positions 50-150 meters in front of the dragon (y: 10-40, x: -30~30, z in front of the dragon);
(11) A ring is considered passed when the dragon's center passes through the ring's center (distance < 3.5 units): score +10, the ring disappears, a brief glowing expansion effect is played, and a new ring is generated at a new random position at the same time;
(12) Rings that have already been passed must not be counted again;

[Terrain]
(13) Use a large plane (500×500) at y=0 with a green material for the ground. Simple noise or a displacement map may optionally be used to create an undulating effect;
(14) If the dragon's altitude y < 1, treat it as a ground crash, deduct 5 points, reset the dragon to (0, 20, 0), and restore its initial orientation;

[Camera]
(15) Third-person tracking camera: position it behind and above the dragon at an approximate relative offset of (0, 5, -15), smoothly following the dragon's position and rotation;

[HUD and State Exposure]
(16) The HUD in the upper-left corner displays:
  - Speed (id="speed", m/s, to 1 decimal place)
  - Altitude (id="altitude", current y coordinate, to 1 decimal place)
  - Score (id="score")
  - Rings passed (id="ringsHit")
  - Elapsed time (id="time", seconds, to 1 decimal place)

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
{
  modelLoaded: boolean,    // Whether the dragon model has finished loading
  speed: number,           // Current flight speed in m/s
  altitude: number,        // Current altitude y
  score: number,           // Current score
  ringsHit: number,        // Number of rings passed
  activeRings: number,     // Number of rings currently in the scene
  time: number,            // Elapsed time (seconds)
  dragonPos: {x:number, y:number, z:number},  // Dragon position
  crashed: boolean         // Whether the dragon was just reset after crashing
}

Output a single HTML file, place it in the same directory as the assets/ folder, and run it through a local HTTP server.
